# Windows batch files keep CRLF line endings in every checkout, including
# Linux and macOS ones. cmd.exe reads batch files with CRLF assumptions:
# LF-only copies can mis-parse labels, GOTO targets, and long lines.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# *.sh and *.py are deliberately not pinned to LF here. TheKnowledge's
# drift report compares managed starter files (install.sh, the stage-2
# scripts, and so on) byte for byte against the TheKnowledge working tree,
# which has no .gitattributes of its own, so an eol=lf rule here would make
# every managed script report as drifted on Windows checkouts that use
# core.autocrlf. The generated files that POSIX shells read at run time
# (bin/claude-local and its peers) are written by claude-wrangler with
# explicit LF line endings instead.
